 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Replacement Policy
+
+The cache uses 2Q. Two FIFO queues hold admitted keys: a probationary queue `A1in` for first-use entries and a protected queue `Am` for entries accessed again after admission. A newly completed sheet is admitted into `A1in`. A request that hits an `A1in` entry promotes it into `Am`; a hit on an `Am` entry leaves it in `Am`. Eviction takes victims from `A1in` before `Am`.
+
+A long sequential screening fills `A1in` with one-use sheets and churns that queue, while a comparison sheet that editors revisit is promoted into `Am` and survives the scan. The boundary between the two queues is a tunable size split (see Open Questions). All queue membership and counters are private to the worker; no replacement decision is visible through the preview interface, and the render queue, job-ticket semantics, and 12 GiB bound are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q with a probationary queue (`A1in`) and a protected queue (`Am`) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | A long sequential screening must not evict the comparison working set editors revisit. 2Q isolates one-use scan sheets in `A1in` and promotes reused sheets into `Am`, so a scan cannot displace comparisons. LRU has the lowest bookkeeping cost and adapts quickly to a project switch, but a sequential scan evicts the working set. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter and aging work and keep yesterday's favorites around past a project switch. The extra queues and a tunable size split are accepted to protect comparison sheets during big screenings. |
+
